      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Somewhere between $1,200 and $1,800 a treadmill stops being a machine you can jog on and
          becomes one you can run on. The specification that changes is not the motor — it is the
          combination of a 60-inch deck and enough frame mass to keep it still underneath you.
        </p>

        <div id="what-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What This Tier Buys That the Last One Did Not</h2>
<p>
            The step from $1,000 to $2,000 is the second genuine value threshold in the treadmill
            market, after the one around $700. Four things change together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A 60-inch deck becomes standard rather than exceptional.</strong> Below this
            band a 60-inch belt appears occasionally and at the cost of everything else. Here it is
            the default, and it is the specification that makes running feel like running instead
            of like managing a machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembled weight passes 250 lbs.</strong> This is what stops the frame moving
            at 9 to 10 mph, and it is the most consistent difference on paper between this band
            and the one below.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck width reaches 22 inches on several machines.</strong> A comfort
            specification rather than a safety one, and one that matters on runs past the hour as
            foot placement wanders with fatigue.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decks are built to last.</strong> Cheaper decks typically soften with
            mileage; machines in this band use heavier decks and
            construction intended to keep the same feel for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-does-not-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What It Still Does Not Buy</h2>
<p>
            Being clear about the ceiling saves people from expecting the next tier's benefits at
            this price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An AC motor.</strong> Every machine here uses a DC motor. AC motors — which
            handle sustained load without the heat build-up that eventually kills DC units — start
            around $3,000. For a home user running an hour a day, a good DC motor is entirely
            adequate; for two people running high mileage daily, the difference becomes real over
            years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial roller diameters.</strong> Expect 2.5-inch rollers here against 3
            inches or more on commercial machines. Larger rollers flex the belt less on each
            rotation and load their bearings less, which is a longevity difference rather than a
            feel one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Freedom from the subscription question.</strong> Most machines in this band are
            built around a content ecosystem, and the ones that are not are the value picks here for
            exactly that reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            If those matter to you, our
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">over-$2,500 guide</a>
            covers where they begin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="total-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Five-Year Cost Changes the Ranking</h2>
<p>
            This is the price band where subscriptions distort comparisons most, because the
            recurring cost is a large fraction of the purchase price rather than a rounding error.
          </p>
          <p>
            A $1,300 subscription-free machine costs $1,300 over five years. An $1,800 machine with
            a $39 monthly subscription costs roughly $4,150 — more than three times as much. Two
            machines that look $500 apart on a comparison page are $2,850 apart in reality, and
            nothing in the hardware accounts for that.
          </p>
          <p>
            This does not mean the connected machines are bad value. If you will genuinely use
            guided sessions several times a week, the content is often what keeps people training,
            and the honest comparison then is against a gym membership rather than against a plain
            treadmill. The argument here is only that the cost belongs in the comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical test is simple: would you pay $39 a month for this content if the machine
            were free? If yes, buy the connected treadmill and enjoy it. If you hesitate, buy the
            subscription-free machine and put a tablet in front of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How We Chose</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. They were chosen and ranked for running from the manufacturers' published
            specifications — deck size, motor rating, incline range and assembled weight —
            together with warranty terms, which are the manufacturer's own statement of how long
            it expects the parts to last.
          </p>
          <p>
            We gave the most weight to deck length and assembled weight. A published belt length
            includes the section curving onto the rear roller, so the surface you can actually run
            on is shorter than the quoted figure, and frame flex is not published at all, which
            leaves machine mass as the most reliable available proxy for how still a frame stays at
            8 to 10 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            Deck consistency is most of what the extra money buys in this band, and it is the
            hardest thing to judge from a specification sheet, which is why we treat assembled
            weight and the parts warranty as the best available proxies for it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-reviews-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard methodology</a>
            explains how we assess machines from published data.
          </p>
        </div>

