            against roughly $4,150 for a connected machine. It gives up screen size and 2 inches of
            belt width, and nothing that matters to how it runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            For hill training specifically, the <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 2450</strong> is
            worth its premium. For everyone else it is the 1750 with a bigger motor you will not
            use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is $2,000 enough for a good running treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Comfortably. This is the band where 60-inch decks become standard, assembled weights pass 250 lbs and decks are built to hold their cushioning. What it does not buy is an AC motor or commercial roller diameters, both of which start around $3,000 and matter mainly for very high mileage or multiple daily users.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a $1,000 and a $2,000 treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainly frame mass and deck consistency. At $1,000 a 60-inch deck usually comes at the cost of frame weight, so the machine is more likely to move at 9 mph and its cushioning to soften with mileage. At $2,000 you get both together — a full deck on a frame that stays still, with deck construction built to keep its feel for years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a treadmill with a subscription at this price?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Count the five-year cost first. A $1,300 subscription-free machine costs $1,300 over five years; an $1,800 machine with a $39 monthly fee costs roughly $4,150. If you would pay $39 a month for the content even if the machine were free, buy the connected one. If you hesitate, buy the plain machine and use a tablet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 22-inch belt or is 20 enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Twenty inches is enough for most runners under about 6'2". Twenty-two gives margin for the wider foot placement that comes with a longer stride and for the way placement wanders as you fatigue, so it matters most on runs past the hour and for taller runners. It is a comfort specification, not a safety one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a treadmill in this price range last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A decade of home use if maintained, four to six years if neglected, and the difference is almost entirely belt lubrication. Rising friction against a dry deck is what wears out motors and controller boards, which are the expensive components. The routine is under an hour a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth spending more than $2,000?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only in three cases: two people running high mileage daily, where an AC motor is a genuinely different class of component; a runner near or above 250 lbs, since capacities here cluster at 300 lbs; or a fifteen-year ownership horizon. Outside those, the money is better spent elsewhere.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

