          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying at This Price</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">At this level the hardware is good enough that the expensive mistakes are about cost structure and specifications rather than build quality.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing purchase prices when one machine has a subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A $1,300 subscription-free machine and an $1,800 connected one look $500 apart and are $2,850 apart over five years. That is more than the price of either treadmill. Run the five-year number before shortlisting, and ask whether you would pay the monthly fee for the content if the hardware were free.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the advertised belt length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Belt figures include the section curving onto the rear roller, which is not usable running surface. The usable length is therefore always shorter than the advertised figure, and how much shorter varies by brand and model — two machines quoting 60 inches need not give you the same running surface. Ask for the flat deck measurement.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying decline you will never use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Downhill running loads the quadriceps eccentrically and is genuinely valuable for preparing for a descent-heavy race. For everyone else it is a mechanism used twice, competing for the same budget as frame mass. Let it be a bonus on a machine you chose for other reasons, never the reason you chose it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not paying for room-of-choice delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">These machines are 250 to 340 lbs in a six-foot crate, and kerbside — a pallet on the driveway — is the default at most retailers. Upgraded delivery costs $100 to $300 and is worth it at this weight. Measure the route including stairwell turns first, because a treadmill cannot be angled the way furniture can.</p>
